<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Week 7 assignment: Infrastructure as Code with OpenTofu on CSC cPouta."
    />
    <title>Week 7 — Infrastructure as Code</title>
    <link href="/index.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  </head>

  <body>
    <main class="document">
      <h1>Week 7 — Infrastructure as Code</h1>

      <section id="part-a">
        <h2>Part A — Questions</h2>

        <article>
          <h3>1. What is Infrastructure as Code?</h3>
          <p>
            Infrastructure as Code (IaC) means describing infrastructure in
            machine-readable files and using tools to create and manage it.
            Instead of repeating console clicks from memory, I can define the
            VM, network port, firewall rules and floating IP as configuration.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the Week 1 server, a manual rebuild meant remembering each
            choice and repeating setup steps. Here, <code>main.tf</code>
            describes the cPouta resources and <code>cloud-init.yaml</code>
            installs Apache and writes the page at first boot. The setup is
            reviewable and repeatable, and a plan makes proposed infrastructure
            changes visible before applying them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Source:
            <a href="https://opentofu.org/docs/intro/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">OpenTofu documentation</a>.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>2. Declarative, imperative and idempotency</h3>
          <p>
            Declarative automation describes the desired end state; the tool
            determines which actions are needed. This OpenTofu configuration
            declares that the VM should use a particular network port and that
            the security group should have specific rules. Imperative
            automation gives ordered commands, such as installing Apache,
            writing the page and then starting the service. The cloud-init
            <code>runcmd</code> section contains imperative steps.
          </p>
          <p>
            Idempotency means that applying the same desired configuration
            repeatedly should converge on the same result instead of creating
            duplicate resources. It helps make retries and drift correction
            safer. It does not mean every change is in-place: a
            replacement-only attribute may still require a resource to be
            recreated.
          </p>
          <p>
            Source:
            <a href="https://opentofu.org/docs/intro/core-workflow/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">OpenTofu core workflow</a>.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>3. What is the state file for?</h3>
          <p>
            OpenTofu state maps resources in the configuration to real cloud
            objects and records attributes needed for later plans. It helps
            OpenTofu determine what exists, what has changed and which
            dependencies matter.
          </p>
          <p>
            State should not be committed to Git because it may contain
            sensitive values and detailed infrastructure metadata. Teams
            usually share state through a secured remote backend with access
            controls, encryption and locking. This exercise uses local state,
            which must remain private and ignored by Git.
          </p>
          <p>
            Source:
            <a href="https://opentofu.org/docs/language/state/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">OpenTofu state documentation</a>.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>4. Provisioning, cloud-init and Ansible</h3>
          <p>
            Provisioning creates infrastructure. In this project, OpenTofu
            requests the key pair, security group, network port, VM and floating
            IP from OpenStack. Configuration management installs software and
            prepares a machine. cloud-init is delivered as VM user data and
            configures the new VM at first boot: it installs Apache, writes
            <code>/var/www/html/index.html</code> and enables the service.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ansible is another configuration-management tool. It is useful for
            repeatable configuration of existing machines and can be combined
            with OpenTofu: provision the VM first, then configure it with
            Ansible. cloud-init is a convenient way to bootstrap a new cloud
            instance.
          </p>
          <p>
            Source:
            <a href="https://cloudinit.readthedocs.io/en/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">cloud-init documentation</a>.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <section id="part-b">
        <h2>Part B — Rebuild the VM as Code</h2>
        <p>
          The OpenTofu configuration looks up the existing CSC project network,
          uploads only my SSH public key, and creates a security group, network
          port, VM and floating IP. The SSH rule is restricted to my IPv4 CIDR;
          HTTP and HTTPS rules allow traffic from the internet. The port
          connects the VM to the project network and security group. The
          floating IP is associated with that port. The project network is a
          data source, so OpenTofu uses it but does not create or destroy it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>versions.tf</code> selects the OpenStack provider.
          <code>variables.tf</code> declares the inputs; the project network,
          SSH CIDR and student name have no defaults so they must be supplied
          deliberately. In particular, SSH should be limited to my own public
          IPv4 address with <code>/32</code>. <code>cloud-init.yaml</code>
          installs Apache and writes the page from the supplied name and
          message. <code>outputs.tf</code> prints the floating IP and page URL.
          <code>terraform.tfvars.example</code> shows the required values;
          personal <code>terraform.tfvars</code>, credentials, private keys and
          state files must not be committed.
        </p>

        <h3>Experiments</h3>
        <p>
          For each experiment, record the prediction before running the
          command, then replace the pending result with what the plan, apply
          and browser actually showed.
        </p>

        <article>
          <h3>1. Change — Option A: page message</h3>
          <p><strong>Prediction:</strong> Changing <code>page_message</code> changes VM <code>user_data</code>. I expect the provider to replace the VM because cloud-init applies user data at first boot. The floating IP is associated with the network port, so I will check whether it stays the same.</p>
          <p><strong>Result:</strong> Pending the cPouta plan and apply. Record the resource counts, the replacement marker and attribute, the page text, and the IP before and after.</p>
          <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Add the observed plan line and explain why the provider handles the changed attribute as it does. Record whether the floating IP changed and that it is attached to the port.</p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>2. Change — Option B: HTTPS rule</h3>
          <p><strong>Prediction:</strong> I expect one security-group rule to be added and the VM to remain unchanged. Opening TCP 443 does not configure Apache with TLS or a certificate, so I do not expect the page to work over HTTPS.</p>
          <p><strong>Result:</strong> Pending the plan, apply and browser check. Record the resource counts and what happened at both HTTP and HTTPS URLs.</p>
          <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Compare the firewall-only change with Option A's VM user-data change. Explain whether allowing port 443 was enough to make HTTPS work.</p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>3. Drift</h3>
          <p><strong>Prediction:</strong> If I manually delete the managed HTTP rule, I expect the plan to detect the missing state-tracked resource and propose recreating it. If I add an undeclared TCP 8080 rule manually, I expect OpenTofu not to remove that unmanaged rule.</p>
          <p><strong>Result:</strong> Pending both plans and browser checks. Record what happened after deleting HTTP, after applying its repair, and after adding TCP 8080.</p>
          <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Explain the difference using the resources recorded in state. Remove the manually added 8080 rule when the experiment is complete.</p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>4. Destroy and rebuild</h3>
          <p><strong>Prediction:</strong> With HTTPS enabled, eight managed resources should be destroyed; the looked-up project network should survive. Dependencies determine the destroy order, and the public pool may assign a different IP after rebuilding.</p>
          <p><strong>Result:</strong> Pending the destroy and apply runs. Record the actual order, whether the project network survived, the new IP, how long rebuilding took, and whether the page worked without manual setup.</p>
          <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Explain the observed order using resource references, why the data-source network remains, and how the output makes a changing IP easy to find.</p>
        </article>

        <h3>Evidence</h3>
        <p>
          Add screenshots in <code>public/evidence/</code>. Use the filenames
          listed below. The page displays an image when its file exists;
          otherwise it shows that the screenshot is still missing. Review each
          image and hide credentials, private keys and personal variable
          values before publishing.
        </p>

        <ol class="evidence-list">
          <% evidence.forEach((item) => { %>
            <li class="evidence-item">
              <strong><%= item.title %>.</strong>
              <%= item.description %>
              <ul class="evidence-files">
                <% item.images.forEach((image) => { %>
                  <li>
                    <code>public/evidence/<%= image.filename %></code>
                    <% if (image.exists) { %>
                      <a class="evidence-image-link" href="<%= image.src %>" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
                        <img class="evidence-image" src="<%= image.src %>" alt="<%= image.alt %>" loading="lazy" />
                      </a>
                    <% } else { %>
                      <span class="missing-evidence">Screenshot not added yet.</span>
                    <% } %>
                  </li>
                <% }); %>
              </ul>
            </li>
          <% }); %>
        </ol>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
